Specifically, the device signature data in ui/signatures.cpp -- the OUI,
service UUID, SSID prefix and company identifier tables, and the per-signature
confidence grading applied to them -- is derived from that project's
src/signatures.cpp and include/signatures.h.

That grading is the part worth crediting. Deciding that a prefix registered to
Espressif is weak evidence for a Flock camera, while one registered to Flock
Safety is strong, is research rather than transcription, and it is what makes
the alerts usable instead of noise.

Changes made: the tables were restructured to UMBRAVOX's own Threat and Conf
enumerations, which are coarser -- several of the upstream detection types are
folded into single buckets, and the Bluetooth Classic name table is omitted
because the ESP32-C5 has no Bluetooth Classic radio.
